WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:05.340
It’s hard to believe it’s been over three whole&nbsp;
months since those monsters came to town. If only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.340 --> 00:00:10.320
I had known back then what they were really&nbsp;
doing here, maybe I could have stopped it. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:15.180
don’t know how, so much of this is beyond me. What&nbsp;
could I have done? Even if I had known about it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:19.500
could I have stopped what happened to&nbsp;
all those people… Sickness, Sarkicism,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:25.560
the SCP Foundation. It’s all still such a blur,&nbsp;
but for a while, I can still remember how I Spent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.560 --> 00:00:30.420
100 Days Infiltrating a Sarkic Cult.
It all began on a pretty ordinary day.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.420 --> 00:00:34.560
I live in a small lumber town, most of&nbsp;
the area is cut off from major cities,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:40.020
connected only by miles and miles of winding road&nbsp;
through the forest. It’s a quiet rural place,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.020 --> 00:00:45.360
and a low-tech way of life. Our main business&nbsp;
has been timber, generations of tree cutters;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:49.740
even in the modern age, we keep that going.
Although my home had always been off the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.740 --> 00:00:54.840
beaten track, we often had our fair share of&nbsp;
visitors. Hikers and wanderers would sometimes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.840 --> 00:00:58.800
stumble upon the town quite by accident,&nbsp;
yet be so enthralled by all the natural&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:02.820
beauty on offer that they’d just have to&nbsp;
spend a night at the only hotel in town,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.820 --> 00:01:08.460
the ‘Woodsman’s Lodge’, run by my friend Andreas.
That’s what we thought they were at first. Just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.460 --> 00:01:13.980
visitors. That’s why we welcomed them when they&nbsp;
arrived. Because not one of us had any idea who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.980 --> 00:01:19.020
these really were… what they really were. We&nbsp;
could never have known that they had chosen our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.020 --> 00:01:25.200
town specifically. They had come here on purpose.
Many of the warier among our town thought these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:31.080
newcomers were strange. They were a group of five,&nbsp;
very obviously monied, but less obvious was their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.080 --> 00:01:35.400
reasons for coming here. When friendly townsfolk&nbsp;
tried to drum up a conversation with any of them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:40.440
they all seemed to report the same thing. That&nbsp;
these strangers were aloof, and each one gave&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.440 --> 00:01:44.820
a dramatically different answer as to why they&nbsp;
had come to our quite little logging community.&nbsp;

00:01:44.820 --> 00:01:49.860
One said their group were just sightseeing,&nbsp;
another said they had gotten lost. Someone else&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.860 --> 00:01:54.900
from their group told a local man they were here&nbsp;
on a mission from someone called Karcist Varis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.900 --> 00:01:59.580
and was rumored to have been quickly silenced&nbsp;
by another in the visitors’ party. But the one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.580 --> 00:02:04.620
thing they all seemingly agreed on was that they&nbsp;
wouldn’t be any trouble. They insisted on that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.620 --> 00:02:08.880
in fact, and said the five of them were&nbsp;
just looking for somewhere to sleep. Bed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.880 --> 00:02:14.040
and board in exchange for cash. And so, someone&nbsp;
pointed them in the direction of Andreas’ hotel.&nbsp;

00:02:14.040 --> 00:02:19.380
I was often there myself, not as a guest, but&nbsp;
helping my friend maintain the place. He’d&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.380 --> 00:02:23.580
inherited the hotel from his family, but&nbsp;
it was always in need of repair. Luckily,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.580 --> 00:02:27.600
my background was in carpentry, and I was more&nbsp;
than happy to help old Andreas out. It wasn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:32.940
one of your fancy, furnished hotels, the kind&nbsp;
you’d find in a big city, all slick and sleek&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.940 --> 00:02:38.880
and owned by a big chain. It was homely, a more&nbsp;
bed-and-breakfast type of place. It was, anyway.&nbsp;

00:02:38.880 --> 00:02:43.800
Whenever I was working over at the Woodsman’s,&nbsp;
I’d observe the strange group of visitors from a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.800 --> 00:02:49.440
distance. They didn’t seem to me like average&nbsp;
tourists or sightseers. To me, they looked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.440 --> 00:02:55.440
wealthy, very wealthy. It wasn’t hard to picture&nbsp;
any one of them as a politician or a businessman,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.440 --> 00:03:00.060
the way they dressed and carried themselves made&nbsp;
it pretty clear they were people of influence.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.060 --> 00:03:04.980
Of course, that begged the question: what on Earth&nbsp;
were people like that doing in a town like this?&nbsp;

00:03:04.980 --> 00:03:09.900
I first got to speak with one of them after a&nbsp;
few days. The five newcomers were down a man,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.900 --> 00:03:14.820
and didn’t seem at all happy about that. The&nbsp;
remaining four were grumpy, looking angered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.820 --> 00:03:19.500
and agitated. I wondered if maybe their fifth&nbsp;
compatriot had left in the night, but when I asked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.500 --> 00:03:24.240
around town, apparently, nobody had seen him.
“Unless he’s crazy and decided to hike through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.240 --> 00:03:27.840
the woods on his own in the middle of&nbsp;
the night,” one of my friends scoffed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.840 --> 00:03:31.260
after telling me that no one had even&nbsp;
heard any cars leaving the night before.&nbsp;

00:03:31.260 --> 00:03:34.860
I decided to ask one of the visitors&nbsp;
when he passed me in the hallway of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.860 --> 00:03:38.100
Woodsman’s Lodge, trying to broach&nbsp;
the topic as tactfully as I could.&nbsp;

00:03:38.100 --> 00:03:42.960
“Sorry to see you’re down a man,” I told him.
He stopped in his tracks, like I’d just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.960 --> 00:03:48.240
caught him doing something he shouldn’t be.
“Yes, yes,” he replied, not turning around to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.240 --> 00:03:54.000
face me. “It’s a terrible shame. A terrible shame&nbsp;
indeed. But he had to leave us. It was for his own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:57.780
good. He knew that. We all do.”
Following that odd encounter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.780 --> 00:04:01.980
things got all the stranger the following&nbsp;
day. I arrived at the Woodsman’s again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.980 --> 00:04:06.240
expecting to start another day of work. I’d&nbsp;
gotten a call from Andreas earlier that morning;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.240 --> 00:04:10.680
he was complaining about some kind of squelching&nbsp;
noise coming from the basement under the hotel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.680 --> 00:04:15.180
that had only started last night. I told him&nbsp;
I wasn’t a plumber, but that I’d take a look.&nbsp;

00:04:15.180 --> 00:04:20.640
So, imagine my surprise when I saw Andreas loading&nbsp;
most of his worldly possessions up into a truck&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.640 --> 00:04:24.600
outside the front of his hotel. He excitedly&nbsp;
explained to me that he’d been bought out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.600 --> 00:04:28.020
that the remaining four newcomers had&nbsp;
offered to buy the Woodsman’s on the spot.&nbsp;

00:04:28.020 --> 00:04:32.580
“But that’s your family’s hotel, Andreas!” I&nbsp;
reminded him, as if it wasn’t already too late.&nbsp;

00:04:32.580 --> 00:04:36.840
“I was never going to be able to keep it afloat&nbsp;
forever,” he shrugged. “When they told me how much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.840 --> 00:04:40.800
they were willing to pay, I couldn’t refuse.&nbsp;
You would have done the same, Ingvar. I have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.800 --> 00:04:43.500
never been offered so much money!”
This all made it very clear:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.500 --> 00:04:47.820
these guests were here to stay.
It had taken less than a week for me to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.820 --> 00:04:53.160
grow suspicious of these people. It wasn’t in my&nbsp;
nature to pry, but so much about them just didn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.160 --> 00:04:58.260
make any sense. They were clearly rich enough to&nbsp;
buy Andreas’ hotel like it was nothing, just an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.260 --> 00:05:03.240
ordinary everyday purchase like buying a loaf of&nbsp;
bread. But they were far too insular to seemingly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.240 --> 00:05:08.100
want to run a hotel for our friendly logging town.
It left me scratching my head over the whole&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.100 --> 00:05:12.420
thing, making the question of what they were&nbsp;
really up to stick out like an unignorable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.420 --> 00:05:17.640
splinter. Were these just careless rich folk&nbsp;
with more money than sense? Had they somehow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.640 --> 00:05:22.320
grown disillusioned with their wealthy affluent&nbsp;
lifestyles and were now using this hotel to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.320 --> 00:05:26.820
learn how to connect with people on a more&nbsp;
fulfilling personal level? Or did they have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.820 --> 00:05:31.860
a different agenda, something else that they&nbsp;
were trying to hide? It turns out, they did…&nbsp;

00:05:31.860 --> 00:05:36.960
I decided to formally introduce myself to the new&nbsp;
owners of the Woodsman’s. After all, I knew the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.960 --> 00:05:41.880
place well; it always needed something repairing.&nbsp;
Plus, if I could keep my little side job of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.880 --> 00:05:45.960
working there as a handyman going, then maybe&nbsp;
I could find out exactly what they were doing.&nbsp;

00:05:45.960 --> 00:05:49.440
I was greeted by the man of their group I’d&nbsp;
spoken to briefly the other day, looking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.440 --> 00:05:54.480
at me with a scowl as I stood in the doorway.
“Hello, sir!” I greeted warmly, met with silence.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.080 --> 00:05:57.660
“We, uh, we met the other day?”
“What do you want?”&nbsp;

00:05:57.660 --> 00:06:03.600
“Oh, well,” I continued, disarmed by the&nbsp;
bluntness. “I understand your group just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.600 --> 00:06:07.680
bought this place. I had a gentlemen’s agreement&nbsp;
with the last owner. I’d help him out, fix things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.680 --> 00:06:11.520
that need fixing. I just thought I should offer&nbsp;
the same service to you if you’d be interested.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.520 --> 00:06:15.360
Might help get this hotel up and running nice&nbsp;
and quick for you, so you can start making a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.360 --> 00:06:17.820
return on your investment.”
With that, the door was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.820 --> 00:06:22.620
unceremoniously slammed in my face.
At first, it certainly seemed like my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.620 --> 00:06:26.700
options for uncovering more about this strange&nbsp;
group were all but depleted. Or at least,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.700 --> 00:06:31.560
doing so through any legitimate means. But&nbsp;
just as I was reaching a desperate point&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.560 --> 00:06:35.700
where I was considering donning a balaclava and&nbsp;
breaking into the hotel in the dead of night,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.700 --> 00:06:41.880
opportunity remerged from behind that closed door.
“We were sorry to turn you away yesterday,”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.880 --> 00:06:46.140
one of the other visitors apologized&nbsp;
somewhat insincerely. “But we would be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.140 --> 00:06:51.060
delighted to take you up on your offer.&nbsp;
Provided you accept a few… conditions.”&nbsp;

00:06:51.060 --> 00:06:56.580
“Conditions?” I echoed. “What would these be?”
“We would need you to start on repairs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.580 --> 00:07:01.680
immediately, we want to open up the hotel’s doors&nbsp;
to guests in the next few days as a matter of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.680 --> 00:07:06.900
urgency,” he explained. “But you will need to&nbsp;
be discreet. Privacy is very important to us,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.900 --> 00:07:12.060
so we hope to maintain that for our guests.”
It all seemed like a lot of hoops to jump through,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.060 --> 00:07:16.560
certainly more than Andreas had given me&nbsp;
when he used to run the Woodsman’s. But it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.560 --> 00:07:20.760
felt like the right call, if it at least&nbsp;
got me closer to answers. In hindsight,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.760 --> 00:07:23.640
I should’ve turned them down…
Over the course of the next week,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.640 --> 00:07:28.140
things seemed to run relatively normally. I’d&nbsp;
be given a checklist of things to fix around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.140 --> 00:07:33.060
the hotel, and for the most part, just focused&nbsp;
purely on the tasks at hand. But whenever the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.060 --> 00:07:37.020
strange new owners were near, I’d try to&nbsp;
subtly listen in on their conversations.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.020 --> 00:07:41.340
They seemed to mostly talk about running the&nbsp;
hotel though, like they knew I was around.&nbsp;

00:07:41.340 --> 00:07:45.900
A few tourists started showing up, in fact,&nbsp;
it seemed to get much busier than usual very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.900 --> 00:07:50.100
quickly. I asked one of the odd owners about&nbsp;
it, and he just brushed it off as the result&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.100 --> 00:07:54.420
of their group marketing the Woodsman’s Lodge&nbsp;
more widely on the Internet. I didn’t see much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.420 --> 00:07:57.960
of the hotel’s guests, staying out of&nbsp;
their way as I had been instructed to.&nbsp;

00:07:57.960 --> 00:08:03.780
But it did strike me as odd how few of them&nbsp;
seemed to leave. Some I spotted around town,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.780 --> 00:08:08.280
usually those traveling as a couple or in a&nbsp;
larger group. Any that were staying alone though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.280 --> 00:08:14.040
they didn’t seem to venture beyond the Woodsman’s.&nbsp;
None of them checked out either, but their rooms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.040 --> 00:08:18.960
would be empty, and often needed repairs –&nbsp;
mainly scratch marks on the wooden floorboards.&nbsp;

00:08:18.960 --> 00:08:22.620
Two and a half weeks after the weird group&nbsp;
had arrived and bought up my friend’s hotel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.620 --> 00:08:25.980
I got a call from someone I knew who&nbsp;
lived next door to the Woodsman’s.&nbsp;

00:08:25.980 --> 00:08:29.640
“The hotel, someone was screaming!”&nbsp;
she said frantically over the line.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.640 --> 00:08:33.360
“Get here quick, they might need you!”
I checked the time; it was the dead of night,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.360 --> 00:08:37.380
so late that it was almost the start of the&nbsp;
next day. Racing quickly over to the hotel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.380 --> 00:08:41.700
I was greeted by one of the owners. He had rushed&nbsp;
to the door, wide awake like he hadn’t even been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.700 --> 00:08:45.720
sleeping. When I told him there were reports&nbsp;
of blood-curdling screams, he reprimanded me.&nbsp;

00:08:45.720 --> 00:08:50.940
“You were told, our guests’ privacy must be&nbsp;
maintained!” he scolded. “If you must know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.940 --> 00:08:55.140
one of the hikers staying with us tripped over&nbsp;
and had a nasty fall. He landed downstairs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.140 --> 00:09:00.000
in the basement, and he’s in a lot of pain, not&nbsp;
to be moved. We’ve called for an ambulance.”&nbsp;

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:04.140
I wanted to take what he had said at face value,&nbsp;
but the fact he wouldn’t let me in to see the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.140 --> 00:09:08.580
injured man felt suspicious. I spent the night&nbsp;
with the person who lived next door to the hotel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.580 --> 00:09:13.620
watching the Woodsman’s Lodge for any sign&nbsp;
of an ambulance. It never arrived. That was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.620 --> 00:09:18.060
when I remembered: he had said the guest&nbsp;
fell into the basement. The same basement&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.060 --> 00:09:22.560
Andreas had heard a strange squelching&nbsp;
coming from. And not once had these new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.560 --> 00:09:27.300
owners asked me to take a look down there…
Already I was even further suspicious of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.300 --> 00:09:31.740
new owners now. I had no idea what they were&nbsp;
up to, the wild theories in my head ranged&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.740 --> 00:09:36.720
from them being a group of sadistic serial&nbsp;
killers using the hotel to lure victims, or a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.720 --> 00:09:42.480
shadowy government black ops team up to something&nbsp;
nefarious. Enough was enough, I needed answers.&nbsp;

00:09:42.480 --> 00:09:47.160
I couldn’t wait for an opportunity, I had to&nbsp;
create one. While as few people were in the hotel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.160 --> 00:09:52.380
as possible, I snuck into the main office. Not&nbsp;
to steal anything or even peek just yet, but to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.380 --> 00:09:56.520
break the legs off a desk; if they asked me to&nbsp;
fix it, then I would be able to spend longer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.520 --> 00:10:01.200
in the office with no supervision. Sure enough,&nbsp;
they soon asked me I’d be able to fix the desk,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.200 --> 00:10:06.180
and that was my in. I started rummaging through&nbsp;
the filing cabinets, rifling through whatever&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.180 --> 00:10:11.040
I could find. Old expenses reports, legal&nbsp;
documents left behind by Andreas, but there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.040 --> 00:10:16.020
was nothing that could give me any inkling as&nbsp;
to what was actually going on. Until there was…&nbsp;

00:10:16.020 --> 00:10:21.120
You see, as much as it came as a surprise&nbsp;
to no one more than me, I wasn’t the only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.120 --> 00:10:24.960
one looking into this strange group…
One of the guests entered the office&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.960 --> 00:10:29.160
and quickly realized what he was doing. At&nbsp;
first, he seemed to imply that I was somehow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.160 --> 00:10:33.600
in league with the new owners. But I was able&nbsp;
to talk him around, telling him I just wanted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.600 --> 00:10:37.620
to try to get to the bottom of what they were up&nbsp;
to. That was when he revealed to me who he was.&nbsp;

00:10:37.620 --> 00:10:42.600
“Agent Clericson,” he introduced himself.&nbsp;
“I’m here on behalf of the SCP Foundation.”&nbsp;

00:10:42.600 --> 00:10:47.040
After he brought me up to speed on what the&nbsp;
hell that all meant, we arranged to reconvene&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.040 --> 00:10:51.540
somewhere they wouldn’t overhear us. Meeting up&nbsp;
at the lumber mill, he explained that these four&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.540 --> 00:10:56.700
strangers were a cult, a subsect of a strange&nbsp;
religion I had never heard of called Sarkicism.&nbsp;

00:10:56.700 --> 00:11:01.080
But after Agent Clericson described&nbsp;
their practices, I wouldn’t soon forget;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.080 --> 00:11:06.180
ritualistic sacrifices, cannibalism,&nbsp;
and even far, far worse than that…&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.180 --> 00:11:10.620
it made me feel sick just hearing about it.
Over the next few days, Clericson agreed to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.620 --> 00:11:15.360
let me help in his investigation, offering me the&nbsp;
chance to help the Foundation and make it so that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.360 --> 00:11:19.860
nobody in my remote little logging town would ever&nbsp;
have to suffer the memory of what the Sarkic cult&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.860 --> 00:11:25.560
were up to. Little did I realize he also meant me.
I did my best to spy on the death and disease&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.560 --> 00:11:30.180
worshipping cultists without drawing too much&nbsp;
suspicion and reported my findings back to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.180 --> 00:11:35.040
Foundation agent. But it quickly became clear&nbsp;
that he was unimpressed with my current method&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.040 --> 00:11:40.800
of just eavesdropping, so instead, he suggested a&nbsp;
more direct approach. Clericson gave me a book on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:40.800 --> 00:11:45.480
the Sarkic religion, telling me to memorize as&nbsp;
much as I could and then approach the cultists&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:45.480 --> 00:11:50.460
as if I shared in their twisted beliefs.
Even reading the teachings of Grand Karcist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.460 --> 00:11:54.900
Ion was enough to make my stomach turn. All&nbsp;
the grotesque things that the founder of this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:54.900 --> 00:11:59.760
cult’s beliefs preached in order to achieve&nbsp;
something akin to godhood… it was horrific.&nbsp;

00:11:59.760 --> 00:12:05.220
What was much more horrific, though, was that our&nbsp;
plan went awry. I asked to speak alone with one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:05.220 --> 00:12:09.720
the cultists, and instantly started explaining&nbsp;
that I had observed them for a time and come&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:09.720 --> 00:12:14.700
to realize that we were one and the same. In a&nbsp;
state of panic, I regurgitated anything I could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:14.700 --> 00:12:19.380
still remember from reading about Sarkicism,&nbsp;
their goal to achieve apotheosis through will,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:19.380 --> 00:12:24.540
the consumption of gods, and… sacrifice. But&nbsp;
I must have said something wrong; one of them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:24.540 --> 00:12:29.220
snuck up behind me and knocked me unconscious.
I woke up in a daze, a splitting headache running&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:29.220 --> 00:12:34.920
through my skull. I was in a cage, in a part&nbsp;
of the hotel I hadn’t seen before. It was dark,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:34.920 --> 00:12:39.960
absent of natural light. There were others&nbsp;
too, I recognized one or two of the hotel’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:39.960 --> 00:12:44.580
single guests, also trapped in cages.&nbsp;
Agent Clericson was nowhere to be found.&nbsp;

00:12:44.580 --> 00:12:49.380
The Sarkic cultists spent the next day asking&nbsp;
me how I had found them. I told them the truth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:49.380 --> 00:12:53.340
and answered their questions the only way I&nbsp;
knew how, but I couldn’t make them believe me.&nbsp;

00:12:53.340 --> 00:12:59.460
“Liar!” one spat. “You did your homework, I’ll&nbsp;
admit. But the Foundation is known for working in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:59.460 --> 00:13:06.300
broad strokes, missing out on the finer details.”
“We’re Neo-Sarkites,” another explained. “You&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:06.300 --> 00:13:10.200
were spouting the teachings of the&nbsp;
Proto-Sarkic religion. We believe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:10.200 --> 00:13:15.360
the only way of becoming a god, achieving&nbsp;
the apotheosis, is through sheer will.”&nbsp;

00:13:15.360 --> 00:13:20.640
“And in terms of sacrifice,” one of&nbsp;
the other cultists chimed in. “We’ll&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:20.640 --> 00:13:26.460
sacrifice many in service of us few. But&nbsp;
you’ll soon come to witness more of that.”&nbsp;

00:13:26.460 --> 00:13:32.520
They kept me in the cage for days, without food&nbsp;
and barely any water. I felt so drained of energy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:32.520 --> 00:13:37.500
before long, and I soon realized why no one&nbsp;
had heard the captive guests calling out for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:37.500 --> 00:13:41.280
help. They were so weak that they could&nbsp;
barely gather enough strength to shout,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:41.280 --> 00:13:45.300
and those that did, their voices were too&nbsp;
weak to reach anyone above the basement.&nbsp;

00:13:45.300 --> 00:13:50.580
The cultists came and went all throughout the&nbsp;
day, alternating between running the Woodsman’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:50.580 --> 00:13:56.280
Lodge and coming down into the dark, cramped&nbsp;
and disgusting room below. The stench was foul,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:56.280 --> 00:14:01.380
yet not one of the Sarkic cultists seemed to&nbsp;
mind. They would just talk endlessly about the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:01.380 --> 00:14:05.220
‘new age of Flesh’, before dragging&nbsp;
their next victim out of their cage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:05.220 --> 00:14:10.140
at night and taking them into a back room.
By this point, all the remaining hotel guests&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:10.140 --> 00:14:14.700
that had been caged up with me were gone. I could&nbsp;
never fully see what went on in the other room,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:14.700 --> 00:14:19.260
but I certainly heard a lot. There were&nbsp;
muffled screams, followed by chanting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:19.260 --> 00:14:23.940
in a language I didn’t understand. Then&nbsp;
came the noise of something slithering,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:23.940 --> 00:14:29.100
all the while the cultists – who had gathered in&nbsp;
the room with their captive victim – kept reciting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:29.100 --> 00:14:35.100
their incantations. I had no idea what was going&nbsp;
on in there, but the hotel guests never remerged,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:35.100 --> 00:14:39.660
just the Sarkites. I had read enough about&nbsp;
their rituals to get a pretty good idea…&nbsp;

00:14:39.660 --> 00:14:43.860
I had lost track of how long I’d been held&nbsp;
prisoner when Agent Clericson appeared in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:43.860 --> 00:14:47.460
the basement. The cultists’ backs had been&nbsp;
turned just long enough for him to slip&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:47.460 --> 00:14:52.260
downstairs undetected. For a moment, I thought&nbsp;
I was saved, only for my heart to sink when he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:52.260 --> 00:14:56.400
told me he couldn’t get me out. I pleaded&nbsp;
with him, but he insisted that he needed to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:56.400 --> 00:15:01.500
know exactly what was going on down here. As he&nbsp;
left me on my own, I cursed him under my breath.&nbsp;

00:15:01.500 --> 00:15:07.320
Before much longer, the cultists decided it was&nbsp;
my turn. They dragged me out of my cage and into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:07.320 --> 00:15:13.140
the back room, strapping me to a chair. I had no&nbsp;
strength left to fight back, but I had plenty of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:13.140 --> 00:15:17.940
fear. There were more cages back here, it was&nbsp;
an old storeroom that they were now keeping…&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:17.940 --> 00:15:25.260
nightmares in. The things in the cages were&nbsp;
disgusting abominations, masses of bloody, gooey&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:25.260 --> 00:15:31.620
flesh… flesh that had once been human. I realized&nbsp;
these were the hotel guests, the ones who traveled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:31.620 --> 00:15:36.660
alone, who never checked out… whose fingernails&nbsp;
had left scratches on the floorboards as they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:36.660 --> 00:15:41.280
were dragged out of their rooms by the cultists.
Using a cattle prod, one of the Sarkites started&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:41.280 --> 00:15:46.680
directing a creature towards me. The horrible&nbsp;
mutant was getting closer, an appendage resembling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:46.680 --> 00:15:51.780
an arm reaching out at me. It was going to&nbsp;
turn me into one of them, and I couldn’t stop&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:51.780 --> 00:15:56.220
it. Scared for my life, I mustered the little&nbsp;
energy I had left to try and pull away from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:56.220 --> 00:16:00.840
the sickening creature, but just as it was about&nbsp;
to make contact… someone kicked down the door.&nbsp;

00:16:00.840 --> 00:16:06.000
Agent Clericson had returned with reinforcements,&nbsp;
members of a Mobile Task Force that promptly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:06.000 --> 00:16:11.400
arrested the Sarkic cultists and sealed the room&nbsp;
containing the monsters for the time being. He sat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:11.400 --> 00:16:15.960
down next to me and explained what had gone on,&nbsp;
but I was barely awake enough to keep up with all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:15.960 --> 00:16:22.500
the details. Something about “SCP-610 instances”&nbsp;
and “rare to see them this far out of Siberia”.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:22.500 --> 00:16:27.120
But he promised that the SCP Foundation would be&nbsp;
ordering the creatures to be incinerated soon.&nbsp;

00:16:27.120 --> 00:16:31.080
The Woodsman’s lodge would have to be&nbsp;
burned down as part of the cover story.&nbsp;

00:16:31.080 --> 00:16:36.780
As for me, I just want to forget the whole ordeal.&nbsp;
The Sarkic cult, all the people they turned into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:36.780 --> 00:16:43.620
monsters, the horrifying things they do, the&nbsp;
SCP-610s. All of it, I want it all gone. Luckily,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:43.620 --> 00:16:48.840
Agent Clericson has given me something he says&nbsp;
will help with that. He called it an amnestic…&nbsp;

00:16:48.840 --> 00:16:54.300
Want an anomaly of your own? Check out&nbsp;
www.scpswag.com for high-quality SCP merch!&nbsp;

00:16:54.300 --> 00:16:59.640
Now go and check out “WHAT DOES THIS SECRET SCP&nbsp;
RELIGION BELIEVE IN?” and “I SURVIVED 100 DAYS&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:59.640 --> 00:17:05.580
HIDING FROM THE SCP FOUNDATION... HERE’S WHAT&nbsp;
HAPPENED” for more mysterious SCP offerings!

